<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">Your questions, answered</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Does my phone connect to the Go or to Bravo?</summary>
      <p>To the Go, which sends all the traffic in one data stream. A Go supports three Bluetooth connections. When paired with a Bravo, it uses one connection for Bravo. That leaves room for two phones or apps.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What if the link between the two drops?</summary>
      <p>The Go loses the ADS-B traffic and keeps the ADS-L and FLARM traffic. Its radar shows BRAVO LOST. In a Tango+, Bravo takes over the alarm again.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does my own transponder show as traffic?</summary>
      <p>Not once you set your aircraft&rsquo;s ICAO address on Bravo or on the paired Go. Without it, your own transponder can show as traffic.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can one aircraft show twice?</summary>
      <p>Yes, in the first version. An aircraft that carries both a Go and an ADS-B transponder can show once in ADS-L and once in ADS-B.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does a Tango transmit ADS-B?</summary>
      <p>No. It transmits your position in ADS-L only, through the Go.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does a Tango need a ground station?</summary>
      <p>No. Bravo receives ADS-B in the aircraft. A skyPost station will add traffic and weather around its airfield: <%= link_to_page "roadmap", label: "what comes next" %>.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
